--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attribution of ideas, not of code.

sourcebook borrows three ideas conceptually and reimplements a much smaller
version of each. No code was copied from any of them, and sourcebook depends on
none of them at build time or at run time.

  * Open Notebook (Apache-2.0) -- the retrieve-then-cite shape, heading-aware
    chunking, and the outline-then-transcript podcast structure. sourcebook
    reimplements these as agent instructions and a stdlib CLI rather than as a
    graph, and deliberately changes the provenance model: Open Notebook anchors a
    citation to a record id, so a re-index can orphan it, whereas a sourcebook
    citation is a byte span into an immutable normalized.md and survives any
    re-chunking.

  * A design-rules lineage of anti-slop critique. The machine-checkable half of
    those rules is compiled here into scripts/sourcebook/lint/rules.py with stable
    rule ids and exit codes; the judgment half stays prose in
    skills/sourcebook/reference/visual.md. The rule registry is a dated snapshot of
    2026's defaults, not eternal law, and is versioned like every other algorithm
    in the kit.

  * A citation-apparatus lineage: superscripts anchoring into a numbered source
    ledger, inline confidence marks, and a credits.json for asset provenance.
    sourcebook formalises these into a five-mark vocabulary and a license validator.

The example sources under examples/demo/sources/ are synthetic. Northline Ferries,
the Harbour Transit Authority, the Meridian Card, Calder Transit, Vantis Rail, and
Harbour Trade Weekly are invented for the demo and describe no real organisation.
